# Disable the vestigial built-in ethernet NIC inside my Dell monitor.
#
# The monitor has a USB hub used as a KVM, sharing keyboard/peripherals between
# my laptop dock and my desktop based on the selected input. That KVM hub is the
# source of the deep USB nesting on this machine.
#
# The monitor's built-in RJ45 has never had a cable in it - my wired networking
# uses separate cables to the dock and to the desktop. But the monitor's USB NIC
# is unstable: it re-enumerates repeatedly (on Windows it popped in and out of
# Device Manager over and over, with a connect sound each time). That bus churn
# was implicated in incidents where all USB devices stopped working until a
# reboot. Since the NIC is unused, we unbind it.
#
# NOTE: the match is by USB *port path* (KERNEL), NOT by idVendor/idProduct,
# because this NIC is an RTL8153 (0bda:8153) - the SAME VID:PID as the DOCK's
# ethernet at 2-1.4.4.3, which must stay up. The port path 2-1.1.1.3 is the
# monitor NIC (behind the Generic-hub chain on bus 2). idVendor/idProduct are
# kept as an extra guard.
#
# We DEAUTHORIZE the device (ATTR{authorized}="0") rather than unbinding it.
# Reason: the RTL8153's usb_device binds to the "r8152-cfgselector" driver, not
# the generic "usb" driver, so the old `echo ... > drivers/usb/unbind` trick
# silently failed. authorized=0 is driver-independent and stronger - the device
# loads no driver and creates no netdev. It re-applies on every "add", so a
# re-enumeration storm just gets it deauthorized again each time.
#
# If the dock/monitor cabling or port layout changes, re-check the path with:
#   readlink -f /sys/class/net/eth*/device   (find the 2-1.1.1.3-style path)
#
# To undo this change, remove the file from /etc/udev/rules.d/ and reboot
# (or: echo 1 | sudo tee /sys/bus/usb/devices/2-1.1.1.3/authorized).

ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", KERNEL=="2-1.1.1.3", ATTR{idVendor}=="0bda", ATTR{idProduct}=="8153", ATTR{authorized}="0"
